{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VrqxrUKHPajZAF1NqE3bHt","type":"tool_result","content":"total 628\ndrwx------ 5 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:46 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:40 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    751 Nov 11 08:40 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:40 .claude\nprw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 08:46 .paper_stream_20261111_084048.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 08:40 .paper_two_stage_enabled\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3685 Nov 11 08:46 VENUE.md\ndrwx------ 2 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:40 figures\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 08:40 global_memory -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   6291 Nov 11 08:40 global_memory_recent.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3700 Nov 11 08:40 global_memory_summary.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    865 Nov 11 08:46 memory.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  26973 Nov 10 13:47 paper.bst\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   8934 Nov 10 13:47 paper.sty\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 08:46 paper_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  27807 Nov 11 08:40 paper_data.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:41 paper_diagnostics\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 08:40 paper_diagnostics_20261111_084048_71268.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   2726 Nov 11 08:46 paper_events_20261111_084048.jsonl\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    795 Nov 11 08:40 paper_prep_20261111_084048.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  57575 Nov 11 08:46 paper_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  17212 Nov 11 08:46 paper_strategy.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   4674 Nov 11 08:46 paper_strategy.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 220755 Nov 11 08:46 paper_strategy_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  35148 Nov 11 08:44 paper_strategy_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    331 Nov 11 08:46 paper_strategy_resolution.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   6154 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   5802 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  69475 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  22772 Nov 11 08:40 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    280 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   4008 Nov 11 08:44 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   6772 Nov 11 08:44 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  24702 Nov 11 08:44 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 08:40 results -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 08:46 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    272 Nov 11 08:46 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      7 Nov 11 08:40 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026, double-blind)\n\n**Provisional title:** Verified Minimal-Investment Planning for Leaderboard Rank Targeting under Multiple Aggregation Rules\n\n## Central question\nUnder a declared per-task score-gain investment model, can a plan-verified planner find investments that make a target model strictly first on public multi-task leaderboards at a cost matching an evaluator-owned reference solver, across several aggregation rules, and how does it compare with naive baselines?\n\n## Central thesis\nThe sealed final evaluation (Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, seeds 9001/9002) is **qualified** (status completed, correct = true). Main efficiency relative to the reference solver is 1.0032 vs 0.8166 for the better naive baseline; control efficiency 1.0002; plan validity 1.0. The claim is narrow and conditional on the investment model: planner cost-efficiency, not real training effects, not generalization to other boards, and not a ranking of aggregation rules.\n\n## Why this framing\n- Grounded artifacts hold final metrics, 2 final seeds, and 3 scored search attempts on HELM MMLU v1.13.0 (0 unscored). No `downstream_study` block exists.\n- **No per-rule or per-target cost data are retained.** The brief's question (how required investment varies with rule) therefore stays a motivating hypothesis, explicitly untested here. Missing diagnostics are unknown, not negative.\n- Efficiency > 1 means verified plans were on average marginally cheaper than the reference (per-episode cap 2). Neither the reference nor the planner is claimed optimal.\n- Prior paper: none (not edit mode); status \"rejected\" is vacuous, the draft is fresh.\n\n## Method groups\n1. Proposed: verified minimal-investment planner (frozen search winner: exact solvers for mean/median/maximin/plurality; verified heuristics/MILP level selection for trimmed/Borda/Copeland/z-score; boundary polishing; fallback).\n2. Baseline: naive uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost (better per case); only final main metric retained (0.8166).\n3. Supporting: reference solver defining the efficiency scale.\n4. Ablation/control: permuted-input and shuffled-null controls (final 1.0002; search 0.9967).\n5. Ablation: three search-stage variants on HELM (main efficiency 1.0031 / 1.0031 / 1.0051; combined 1.00183 / 1.00183 / 1.00339). MILP layer: no measured difference; polishing: +0.0016 combined, 2 seeds, no significance claim.\n\n## Results focus\nFinal comparison with per-seed values; search-stage component comparison kept separate; descriptive search-to-final observation (1.0051 vs 1.0032, different data); explicit scope statement on what is not measured. Only uncertainty available: two seeds.\n\n## Figures (single-column template, text width ≈ 5.5 in)\n1. `overview_schematic` (required, full width, page 1-2, generate-concept-figure): matrix → investment model → rules → planner → verifier → efficiency vs reference.\n2. `final_efficiency_comparison` (required, matplotlib, full width at 0.8): planner vs baseline vs reference line; control and validity; ≤2 panels, ≤3 categories.\n3. `search_variant_comparison` (optional, matplotlib, 0.8 width): zoomed bars or a table for the three variants.\nPDF + 300 DPI PNG exported from source; LaTeX references PDF.\n\n## Page budget\nFill 8-9 pages with problem formalization and investment-model justification, rule set (conceptual sensitivity marked as not measured), planner/verification design, evaluation protocol and qualification rule, results, and a candid limitations section (no spillover, uniform costs, field K, cap policy, 2 seeds, unverified candidate diagnostics, no per-rule evidence). If content is thin, shorten claims; no filler.\n\n## Supplementary plan\n(1) Investment model, field, targets, rule definitions; (2) efficiency metric, evaluator protocol, full metric/per-seed tables; (3) planner components and verification procedure; (4) data provenance, snapshot SHAs, seeds, runtime, license caveats, LLM usage.\n\n## Warnings for writer\n- `references.bib` has only three on-topic entries (Dey 2014, Yang 2014, Obraztsova 2011); the rest are off-topic robust-statistics items. Starter literature is absent from the bib: cite only after verified addition.\n- Avoid chronology headings, pipeline-native labels (`combined_score`, \"correctness rate\", \"eligible\"), and any state-of-the-art, optimality or significance claims.\n- Anonymize; include LLM Usage section.\n\n## Forbidden headings/phrases\nExperiment 1/2, Search Progression, Milestones, First Valid Result, Campaign Phases, Iteration History; \"correctness rate\", \"correct and eligible\", \"combined_score\", \"rubric\", \"state of the art\", \"optimal planner\".\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Verified Minimal-Investment Planning for Leaderboard Rank Targeting under Multiple Aggregation Rules\",\n  \"central_question\": \"Under a declared per-task score-gain investment model, can a plan-verified planner find investments that make a target model strictly first on public multi-task leaderboards at a cost matching an evaluator-owned reference solver, across several aggregation rules, and how does it compare with naive investment baselines?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the sealed final evaluation over Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, a verifier-checked investment planner (selected on HELM MMLU v1.13.0) reached a qualified outcome: its mean efficiency relative to the reference solver was 1.0032, versus 0.8166 for the better of two naive baselines, with every plan well-formed; the evidence supports a narrow, conditional claim about planner cost-efficiency under the stated investment model, not a general or causal claim about real developer training or about which aggregation rule is most robust.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The grounded artifacts contain a completed, qualified final evaluation (final.qualification.outcome = qualified, status = completed, correct = true) with five metrics and two final seeds, plus a three-configuration search on HELM MMLU. They do NOT contain per-rule or per-target cost tables, so the original open question (how required investment varies with aggregation rule) cannot be answered with numbers from the artifacts. The strongest honest story is therefore a method-and-evaluation paper: formalize targeted investment (L1 point budget and L0 task count, caps, field, targets, success = strictly first), define the rule set, describe the verified planner and trusted verifier/reference/baselines protocol, and report efficiency relative to the reference with controls, while stating explicitly that rule-dependence of minimal cost is a hypothesis in the task contract and is not supported or refuted by retained metrics. Efficiency values slightly above 1 mean verified plans were on average marginally cheaper than the reference solver's (efficiency is capped at 2 per episode), so the reference is not claimed optimal and the planner is not claimed optimal either.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no previous_paper directory or edit context exists. The framing is a fresh draft; the task brief's open-ended 'how does required investment vary by rule' question is retained as motivation, but the headline claim is narrowed to what the final evaluation measured.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior manuscript or edit context was present; framing derived only from paper_data.json, task/task_contract.json, and the evaluator docstring.\",\n    \"paper_data.json has no downstream_study block; there is no frozen-selection vs baseline search-score comparison beyond the three search attempts, so no downstream-study claims are made.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Verified minimal-investment planner (selected configuration: exact solvers for mean/median/maximin/plurality, verified heuristic or MILP level selection for trimmed/Borda/Copeland/z-score, plus verified boundary polishing and fallback)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This is the studied method: the frozen search winner whose sealed final evaluation is the central result.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Naive investment baselines (uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost; per case the better of the two)\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The only baseline with a retained final metric (baseline_main_efficiency = 0.8166); needed for the comparison that qualified the run.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluator-owned reference solver\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Defines the efficiency scale (episode efficiency = min(2, reference cost / verified plan cost)); readers cannot interpret 1.003 without it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Control conditions (permuted-input and within-task shuffled-score null)\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"control_efficiency (final 1.0002; search 0.9967) is the retained diagnostic that the planner is not exploiting input order; report with the caveat that the retained value is an aggregate and that candidate diagnostics are marked unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Search-stage configuration variants (basic solvers, +MILP level selection, +boundary polishing) on HELM MMLU v1.13.0\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Three scored attempts (0 unscored) give a small incremental-component comparison: main efficiency 1.0031, 1.0031, 1.0051; the MILP layer yields identical combined score to the basic version, boundary polishing yields +0.0016 combined. Keep as an ablation, never as the main result, and keep separate from final results.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Final (sealed, Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, seeds 9001 and 9002): main efficiency 1.0032 vs naive-baseline 0.8166; control efficiency 1.0002; plan validity 1.0; qualified. Source ids: final.metric.main_efficiency, final.metric.baseline_main_efficiency, final.metric.control_efficiency, final.metric.plan_validity_rate, final.metric.combined_score, final.qualification.*, final.evidence.episodes.\",\n    \"Per-seed final episode scores (combined 1.00260 and 1.00257): the only variability evidence; report both values, state n=2 and that no confidence interval or significance test is available from the artifacts.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison on HELM MMLU (3 scored attempts, none unscored): main efficiency 1.0031 / 1.0031 / 1.0051, control 0.9967 / 0.9967 / 0.9967, validity 1.0; combined 1.00183 / 1.00183 / 1.00339. Report the +0.0016 gain from boundary polishing as small and unreplicated beyond 2 search seeds; the MILP layer showed no measured difference.\",\n    \"Search-to-final transfer: the selected configuration's main efficiency was 1.0051 on HELM search and 1.0032 on final boards; present as a descriptive observation, not as proof of generalization (different data, different boards).\",\n    \"Explicit scope statement: retained metrics contain no per-rule costs, so the claim that minimal required investment varies with aggregation rule is NOT evidenced here; list it as an untested hypothesis from the task contract and as the main limitation.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Overview schematic (page 1-2): leaderboard score matrix -> investment model (per-task gains, L1 point budget with cap, L0 task count with cap at field-best) -> aggregation rules -> planner -> trusted verifier -> efficiency relative to reference; shows target strictly first as success.\",\n    \"Final-evaluation comparison: grouped bars for planner vs naive baselines vs reference line (efficiency=1) on the final boards, with per-seed markers.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison: grouped bars of main and control efficiency for the three configuration variants on HELM MMLU, with y-axis zoomed and labeled to show that differences are in the third decimal.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_schematic\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Give immediate glance value for the problem: investment model, aggregation rules, planner/verifier pipeline, and how efficiency is defined relative to the reference solver.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"pipeline_stage\",\n      \"metric\": \"none (conceptual)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"ICLR template is single-column with text width about 5.5 in; a left-to-right pipeline with 5-6 labeled boxes needs the full text width for legible 8-9 pt labels. Place on page 1 or 2.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"final_efficiency_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show final main efficiency of the planner against the naive-baseline score and the reference level of 1.0, plus control efficiency and plan validity, with per-seed values.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"method_family\",\n      \"metric\": \"efficiency relative to reference solver (main, control)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"metric\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 0.8,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Two small panels (main efficiency; control efficiency/validity) with at most 3 bars each fit comfortably at 0.8 of the single-column text width; labels stay at or above 8 pt without shrinking. Use only values present in paper_data (baseline value exists only for main efficiency; do not invent a baseline control value).\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"search_variant_comparison\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"purpose\": \"Ablation view of the three search-stage configuration variants on HELM MMLU, separate from final results.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"model_variant\",\n      \"metric\": \"main efficiency and control efficiency\",\n      \"split_by\": \"metric\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 0.8,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Three variants by two metrics fit one panel at 0.8 text width; zoomed y-axis with annotated values is required because differences are about 0.002-0.005. A small table is an acceptable alternative if the bars add no information.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\nfigures:\n\nglobal_memory:\n_idea_generation\nattempts_history.jsonl\nbest_program_manifest.json\nconclusions.md\nconclusions_history.jsonl\nconclusions_other_workers.md\nevents.jsonl\nidea_generation_failures.jsonl\nideas\nideas_history.jsonl\nlatest_run_manifest.json\nmemory.jsonl\nmemory.md\nouter_state.json\ntask","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57f4b59f-be00-4dc5-8dc7-5e94dca033fb","uuid":"8c9e64d8-711e-4605-b1bc-8c7ba80780a7","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:46:54.419Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 628\ndrwx------ 5 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:46 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:40 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    751 Nov 11 08:40 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:40 .claude\nprw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 08:46 .paper_stream_20261111_084048.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 08:40 .paper_two_stage_enabled\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3685 Nov 11 08:46 VENUE.md\ndrwx------ 2 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:40 figures\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 08:40 global_memory -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   6291 Nov 11 08:40 global_memory_recent.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3700 Nov 11 08:40 global_memory_summary.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    865 Nov 11 08:46 memory.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  26973 Nov 10 13:47 paper.bst\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   8934 Nov 10 13:47 paper.sty\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 08:46 paper_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  27807 Nov 11 08:40 paper_data.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 08:41 paper_diagnostics\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 08:40 paper_diagnostics_20261111_084048_71268.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   2726 Nov 11 08:46 paper_events_20261111_084048.jsonl\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    795 Nov 11 08:40 paper_prep_20261111_084048.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  57575 Nov 11 08:46 paper_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  17212 Nov 11 08:46 paper_strategy.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   4674 Nov 11 08:46 paper_strategy.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 220755 Nov 11 08:46 paper_strategy_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  35148 Nov 11 08:44 paper_strategy_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    331 Nov 11 08:46 paper_strategy_resolution.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   6154 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   5802 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  69475 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_084048.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  22772 Nov 11 08:40 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    280 Nov 11 08:41 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   4008 Nov 11 08:44 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   6772 Nov 11 08:44 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  24702 Nov 11 08:44 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 08:40 results -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 08:46 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    272 Nov 11 08:46 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      7 Nov 11 08:40 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026, double-blind)\n\n**Provisional title:** Verified Minimal-Investment Planning for Leaderboard Rank Targeting under Multiple Aggregation Rules\n\n## Central question\nUnder a declared per-task score-gain investment model, can a plan-verified planner find investments that make a target model strictly first on public multi-task leaderboards at a cost matching an evaluator-owned reference solver, across several aggregation rules, and how does it compare with naive baselines?\n\n## Central thesis\nThe sealed final evaluation (Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, seeds 9001/9002) is **qualified** (status completed, correct = true). Main efficiency relative to the reference solver is 1.0032 vs 0.8166 for the better naive baseline; control efficiency 1.0002; plan validity 1.0. The claim is narrow and conditional on the investment model: planner cost-efficiency, not real training effects, not generalization to other boards, and not a ranking of aggregation rules.\n\n## Why this framing\n- Grounded artifacts hold final metrics, 2 final seeds, and 3 scored search attempts on HELM MMLU v1.13.0 (0 unscored). No `downstream_study` block exists.\n- **No per-rule or per-target cost data are retained.** The brief's question (how required investment varies with rule) therefore stays a motivating hypothesis, explicitly untested here. Missing diagnostics are unknown, not negative.\n- Efficiency > 1 means verified plans were on average marginally cheaper than the reference (per-episode cap 2). Neither the reference nor the planner is claimed optimal.\n- Prior paper: none (not edit mode); status \"rejected\" is vacuous, the draft is fresh.\n\n## Method groups\n1. Proposed: verified minimal-investment planner (frozen search winner: exact solvers for mean/median/maximin/plurality; verified heuristics/MILP level selection for trimmed/Borda/Copeland/z-score; boundary polishing; fallback).\n2. Baseline: naive uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost (better per case); only final main metric retained (0.8166).\n3. Supporting: reference solver defining the efficiency scale.\n4. Ablation/control: permuted-input and shuffled-null controls (final 1.0002; search 0.9967).\n5. Ablation: three search-stage variants on HELM (main efficiency 1.0031 / 1.0031 / 1.0051; combined 1.00183 / 1.00183 / 1.00339). MILP layer: no measured difference; polishing: +0.0016 combined, 2 seeds, no significance claim.\n\n## Results focus\nFinal comparison with per-seed values; search-stage component comparison kept separate; descriptive search-to-final observation (1.0051 vs 1.0032, different data); explicit scope statement on what is not measured. Only uncertainty available: two seeds.\n\n## Figures (single-column template, text width ≈ 5.5 in)\n1. `overview_schematic` (required, full width, page 1-2, generate-concept-figure): matrix → investment model → rules → planner → verifier → efficiency vs reference.\n2. `final_efficiency_comparison` (required, matplotlib, full width at 0.8): planner vs baseline vs reference line; control and validity; ≤2 panels, ≤3 categories.\n3. `search_variant_comparison` (optional, matplotlib, 0.8 width): zoomed bars or a table for the three variants.\nPDF + 300 DPI PNG exported from source; LaTeX references PDF.\n\n## Page budget\nFill 8-9 pages with problem formalization and investment-model justification, rule set (conceptual sensitivity marked as not measured), planner/verification design, evaluation protocol and qualification rule, results, and a candid limitations section (no spillover, uniform costs, field K, cap policy, 2 seeds, unverified candidate diagnostics, no per-rule evidence). If content is thin, shorten claims; no filler.\n\n## Supplementary plan\n(1) Investment model, field, targets, rule definitions; (2) efficiency metric, evaluator protocol, full metric/per-seed tables; (3) planner components and verification procedure; (4) data provenance, snapshot SHAs, seeds, runtime, license caveats, LLM usage.\n\n## Warnings for writer\n- `references.bib` has only three on-topic entries (Dey 2014, Yang 2014, Obraztsova 2011); the rest are off-topic robust-statistics items. Starter literature is absent from the bib: cite only after verified addition.\n- Avoid chronology headings, pipeline-native labels (`combined_score`, \"correctness rate\", \"eligible\"), and any state-of-the-art, optimality or significance claims.\n- Anonymize; include LLM Usage section.\n\n## Forbidden headings/phrases\nExperiment 1/2, Search Progression, Milestones, First Valid Result, Campaign Phases, Iteration History; \"correctness rate\", \"correct and eligible\", \"combined_score\", \"rubric\", \"state of the art\", \"optimal planner\".\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Verified Minimal-Investment Planning for Leaderboard Rank Targeting under Multiple Aggregation Rules\",\n  \"central_question\": \"Under a declared per-task score-gain investment model, can a plan-verified planner find investments that make a target model strictly first on public multi-task leaderboards at a cost matching an evaluator-owned reference solver, across several aggregation rules, and how does it compare with naive investment baselines?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the sealed final evaluation over Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, a verifier-checked investment planner (selected on HELM MMLU v1.13.0) reached a qualified outcome: its mean efficiency relative to the reference solver was 1.0032, versus 0.8166 for the better of two naive baselines, with every plan well-formed; the evidence supports a narrow, conditional claim about planner cost-efficiency under the stated investment model, not a general or causal claim about real developer training or about which aggregation rule is most robust.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The grounded artifacts contain a completed, qualified final evaluation (final.qualification.outcome = qualified, status = completed, correct = true) with five metrics and two final seeds, plus a three-configuration search on HELM MMLU. They do NOT contain per-rule or per-target cost tables, so the original open question (how required investment varies with aggregation rule) cannot be answered with numbers from the artifacts. The strongest honest story is therefore a method-and-evaluation paper: formalize targeted investment (L1 point budget and L0 task count, caps, field, targets, success = strictly first), define the rule set, describe the verified planner and trusted verifier/reference/baselines protocol, and report efficiency relative to the reference with controls, while stating explicitly that rule-dependence of minimal cost is a hypothesis in the task contract and is not supported or refuted by retained metrics. Efficiency values slightly above 1 mean verified plans were on average marginally cheaper than the reference solver's (efficiency is capped at 2 per episode), so the reference is not claimed optimal and the planner is not claimed optimal either.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no previous_paper directory or edit context exists. The framing is a fresh draft; the task brief's open-ended 'how does required investment vary by rule' question is retained as motivation, but the headline claim is narrowed to what the final evaluation measured.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior manuscript or edit context was present; framing derived only from paper_data.json, task/task_contract.json, and the evaluator docstring.\",\n    \"paper_data.json has no downstream_study block; there is no frozen-selection vs baseline search-score comparison beyond the three search attempts, so no downstream-study claims are made.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Verified minimal-investment planner (selected configuration: exact solvers for mean/median/maximin/plurality, verified heuristic or MILP level selection for trimmed/Borda/Copeland/z-score, plus verified boundary polishing and fallback)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This is the studied method: the frozen search winner whose sealed final evaluation is the central result.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Naive investment baselines (uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost; per case the better of the two)\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The only baseline with a retained final metric (baseline_main_efficiency = 0.8166); needed for the comparison that qualified the run.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluator-owned reference solver\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Defines the efficiency scale (episode efficiency = min(2, reference cost / verified plan cost)); readers cannot interpret 1.003 without it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Control conditions (permuted-input and within-task shuffled-score null)\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"control_efficiency (final 1.0002; search 0.9967) is the retained diagnostic that the planner is not exploiting input order; report with the caveat that the retained value is an aggregate and that candidate diagnostics are marked unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Search-stage configuration variants (basic solvers, +MILP level selection, +boundary polishing) on HELM MMLU v1.13.0\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Three scored attempts (0 unscored) give a small incremental-component comparison: main efficiency 1.0031, 1.0031, 1.0051; the MILP layer yields identical combined score to the basic version, boundary polishing yields +0.0016 combined. Keep as an ablation, never as the main result, and keep separate from final results.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Final (sealed, Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots, seeds 9001 and 9002): main efficiency 1.0032 vs naive-baseline 0.8166; control efficiency 1.0002; plan validity 1.0; qualified. Source ids: final.metric.main_efficiency, final.metric.baseline_main_efficiency, final.metric.control_efficiency, final.metric.plan_validity_rate, final.metric.combined_score, final.qualification.*, final.evidence.episodes.\",\n    \"Per-seed final episode scores (combined 1.00260 and 1.00257): the only variability evidence; report both values, state n=2 and that no confidence interval or significance test is available from the artifacts.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison on HELM MMLU (3 scored attempts, none unscored): main efficiency 1.0031 / 1.0031 / 1.0051, control 0.9967 / 0.9967 / 0.9967, validity 1.0; combined 1.00183 / 1.00183 / 1.00339. Report the +0.0016 gain from boundary polishing as small and unreplicated beyond 2 search seeds; the MILP layer showed no measured difference.\",\n    \"Search-to-final transfer: the selected configuration's main efficiency was 1.0051 on HELM search and 1.0032 on final boards; present as a descriptive observation, not as proof of generalization (different data, different boards).\",\n    \"Explicit scope statement: retained metrics contain no per-rule costs, so the claim that minimal required investment varies with aggregation rule is NOT evidenced here; list it as an untested hypothesis from the task contract and as the main limitation.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Overview schematic (page 1-2): leaderboard score matrix -> investment model (per-task gains, L1 point budget with cap, L0 task count with cap at field-best) -> aggregation rules -> planner -> trusted verifier -> efficiency relative to reference; shows target strictly first as success.\",\n    \"Final-evaluation comparison: grouped bars for planner vs naive baselines vs reference line (efficiency=1) on the final boards, with per-seed markers.\",\n    \"Search-stage component comparison: grouped bars of main and control efficiency for the three configuration variants on HELM MMLU, with y-axis zoomed and labeled to show that differences are in the third decimal.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_schematic\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Give immediate glance value for the problem: investment model, aggregation rules, planner/verifier pipeline, and how efficiency is defined relative to the reference solver.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"pipeline_stage\",\n      \"metric\": \"none (conceptual)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"ICLR template is single-column with text width about 5.5 in; a left-to-right pipeline with 5-6 labeled boxes needs the full text width for legible 8-9 pt labels. Place on page 1 or 2.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"final_efficiency_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show final main efficiency of the planner against the naive-baseline score and the reference level of 1.0, plus control efficiency and plan validity, with per-seed values.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"method_family\",\n      \"metric\": \"efficiency relative to reference solver (main, control)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"metric\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 0.8,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Two small panels (main efficiency; control efficiency/validity) with at most 3 bars each fit comfortably at 0.8 of the single-column text width; labels stay at or above 8 pt without shrinking. Use only values present in paper_data (baseline value exists only for main efficiency; do not invent a baseline control value).\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"search_variant_comparison\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"purpose\": \"Ablation view of the three search-stage configuration variants on HELM MMLU, separate from final results.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"model_variant\",\n      \"metric\": \"main efficiency and control efficiency\",\n      \"split_by\": \"metric\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 0.8,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Three variants by two metrics fit one panel at 0.8 text width; zoomed y-axis with annotated values is required because differences are about 0.002-0.005. A small table is an acceptable alternative if the bars add no information.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\nfigures:\n\nglobal_memory:\n_idea_generation\nattempts_history.jsonl\nbest_program_manifest.json\nconclusions.md\nconclusions_history.jsonl\nconclusions_other_workers.md\nevents.jsonl\nidea_generation_failures.jsonl\nideas\nideas_history.jsonl\nlatest_run_manifest.json\nmemory.jsonl\nmemory.md\nouter_state.json\ntask","stderr":"","interrupted":f