{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvsTM7KEzJC1fztC4ej7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WGbjbrtuXNi6bhGiUVz4H4","name":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\n\nrationale = r\"\"\"\\subsection{Design rationale and hypotheses}\nThree considerations shaped the planner, and each yields a hypothesis that the evaluation can address in part.\n\\emph{Verification before return.} Discrete rules flip at exact floating-point ties, and heuristics that shave a plan to the boundary can land on the wrong side by rounding. Returning only plans that an exact replica of the rule confirms removes this failure mode at the cost of extra evaluations, and it makes the planner's output trustworthy independently of the solver that proposed it. \\emph{Rule-specific structure.} A uniform gain ignores which tasks are cheap to lift; solvers that exploit the structure of each rule (Table~\\ref{tab:rules}) should find cheaper plans than a rule-agnostic fill. \\emph{Boundary polishing.} A plan returned by a constructive heuristic is rarely tight, so shrinking each gain to the verification boundary, dropping redundant tasks and moving points between tasks should lower cost further, and for L0 two-for-one swaps can reduce the number of treated tasks. We state three hypotheses:\n\\begin{description}\n\\item[H1 (solver advantage).] Verified per-rule solvers have higher main efficiency than the better of uniform-gain and greedy marginal-cost baselines on held-out boards.\n\\item[H2 (polish).] Boundary polishing lowers verified plan cost relative to the base solvers on a development leaderboard.\n\\item[H3 (MILP selection).] A MILP level-selection layer lowers cost for the discrete rules (Borda, Copeland, plurality) and the trimmed mean relative to the base solvers.\n\\end{description}\nH1 is the only hypothesis tested on held-out data. H2 and H3 are tested only in the development comparison with two seeds. The question of rule-dependence in required investment is not among the tested hypotheses (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\n\"\"\"\ns=s.replace(\"\\\\subsection{Verification-first planner}\\\\label{sec:planner}\", rationale+\"\\\\subsection{Verification-first planner}\\\\label{sec:planner}\")\n\nreading = r\"\"\"\\paragraph{Reading an efficiency near 1.} The held-out main efficiency averages 64 main cases per seed (8 rules, 2 target ranks, 2 cost measures, 2 boards), each with weight $1/64\\approx0.0156$. An excess of 0.0032 over 1 is therefore about one fifth of the credit a single case would add at the maximal per-episode efficiency of 2, and about 0.2 of a case-unit in total. The retained aggregate cannot distinguish many tiny savings relative to the reference from a few larger savings offset by some losses; this decomposition is unknown.\n\n\"\"\"\ns=s.replace(\"\\\\subsection{Controls and diagnostics}\", reading+\"\\\\subsection{Controls and diagnostics}\")\n\nstatus = r\"\"\"\\subsection{Status of the hypotheses}\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Evidential status of the hypotheses and of the motivating question. ``Not measured'' means no retained metric addresses the statement.}\n\\label{tab:status}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.3cm}p{1.9cm}p{4.0cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\nStatement & Status & Evidence and scope\\\\\n\\midrule\nH1: solvers beat naive baselines & Supported & Held-out main efficiency 1.0032 vs.\\ 0.8166; two boards, two seeds; planner pool includes the naive plan\\\\\nH2: polish lowers cost & Small gain, unreplicated & Development combined 1.00339 vs.\\ 1.00183; two seeds; about 4$\\times$ evaluator wall-clock\\\\\nH3: MILP layer lowers cost & Not supported & Development metrics identical to base; cause not recoverable\\\\\nRequired investment varies by rule & Not measured & No per-rule costs retained\\\\\nGeneralization to other boards or real training & Not measured & Outside the declared model\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\"\"\"\ns=s.replace(\"\\\\subsection{What these results do not show}\", status+\"\\\\subsection{What these results do not show}\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE\n(pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/tmp/l1.log; bibtex paper >/tmp/b.log; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/tmp/l2.log; pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode paper.tex >/tmp/l3.log); grep -n \"Error\\|undefined\" /tmp/l3.log | head; $TMPDIR/pyenv/bin/python - <<'E' 2>/dev/null\nimport pymupdf\nd=pymupdf.open('/workspace/paper/paper.pdf'); print(len(d))\nfor n in range(1,len(d)+1):\n    p=d[n-1]; bl=[b for b in p.get_text('blocks') if b[3]<740 and 'Generated by' not in b[4]]\n    print(n,p.get_text()[40:90].replace('\\n',' '), round(max(b[3] for b in bl)))\nE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1550,"cache_read_input_tokens":166931,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1550},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57f4b59f-be00-4dc5-8dc7-5e94dca033fb","uuid":"30095dda-b6f1-4ab1-a211-932b57455f72","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:03:28.026Z","request_id":"req_011CgvsTKcmWskgeqAEkJw1f","tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WGbjbrtuXNi6bhGiUVz4H4","display_name":"Bash"}]}
